Title:
Breaking ETW (Event Tracing for Windows) and EDR

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains how attackers can tamper with Windows user-mode ETW telemetry that many EDR/XDR products rely on for behavioral detection.  
- It focuses on practical manipulation of ETW tracing sessions and providers—creating sessions, enabling/disabling providers/keywords, and stopping sessions—primarily using the built-in `logman.exe` controller.  
- The article demonstrates collecting process and image-load telemetry via the `Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process` provider and inspecting resulting `.etl` logs in Event Viewer, then shows how providers/sessions can be removed or killed.  
- It also includes a minimal C# ETW consumer example using `Microsoft.Diagnostics.Tracing` / `TraceEvent` to subscribe to kernel process and image-load events.  
- A key caveat is that these techniques target user-mode ETW providers and do not bypass kernel ETW Threat Intelligence (ETW-TI), which is harder to tamper with and typically requires higher privileges to interfere with.  
- Useful for red teamers/pentesters learning ETW-based visibility gaps and for defenders building detections around ETW session/provider tampering.

Technical Focus:
- ETW architecture (providers, consumers, controllers, sessions, keywords)
- `logman.exe` session management (create/query/update/stop)
- Provider/keyword selection (e.g., `Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process`, process/image keywords)
- `.etl` trace collection and inspection (Event Viewer)
- ETW consumption in C# via TraceEvent (`TraceEventSession`, `KernelTraceEventParser`)
- Limitations vs ETW-TI (kernel-mode telemetry)

Use Cases:
- Enumerate and manipulate ETW sessions/providers to reduce user-mode telemetry visibility
- Build custom ETW collectors/consumers for process and module-load monitoring
- Validate what an EDR could be seeing from specific ETW providers/keywords
- Develop detections for suspicious ETW session stops/provider removals
- Lab testing of ETW-based detection engineering and telemetry resilience

Keywords:
ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, EDR, XDR, logman.exe, tracing session, ETW provider, ETW consumer, ETW controller, keywords, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process, WINEVENT_KEYWORD_PROCESS, WINEVENT_KEYWORD_IMAGE, .etl, Event Viewer, TraceEvent, Microsoft.Diagnostics.Tracing, KernelTraceEventParser, ETW-TI